# Netlify reads the first rule that matches, top to bottom, so the order here is
# the whole point: a missing recording answers 404 instead of falling through to
# the app document below.
#
# That fall-through is what broke the audiobooks on individual phones. Between the
# November 16 and November 18 deploys the /media folder was not in the publish
# directory, so every request for an mp3 was answered with index.html and a 200,
# and the /media cache header in netlify.toml told the browser to keep that
# document for thirty days. A browser caches by url, not by device, so the handsets
# that tried to listen in that window kept replaying their own cached copy of the
# app and heard nothing, while every other device was fine. A 404 cannot do that:
# it is not a playable file, and the player reports it as a failure instead.
#
# Existing files are never shadowed by a rule that is not forced, so all 18
# recordings still come from disk with byte ranges.
/media/*    /404.html    404

# Single page app: deep links like /dashboard/courses resolve to the document and
# React takes the route from there.
/*          /index.html   200
